  "body": "I have been looking into Web Components and it looks like a big step forward. The combination of html imports and html templates have good potential, but I was a bit surprised that the actual example use is thru (java)scripting.\n\nI would suggest to eleminate the _NEED_ for this by introducing some small html improvements. This way developers are free to decide where to implement their logic. There's some similarity with XSL's approach.\n- the possibility to apply templates, for example:\n  `<apply template=\"name-template\">`\n  But also include would be a good name (with more options).\n- the possibility to pass data when applying a template, for example:\n  `<apply template=\"name-template\" data-name=\"rdewilde\">`\n- the possibilty to access the passed data, for example:\n  `<template id=\"name-template\">My name is <data ref=\"name\" />! </template>`\n- optional, the possibility to define the data-attributes a template accepts (which could help auto-completion etc). For example:\n  `<template id=\"name-template\" data-name>... </template>`\n- optional, the possibility to couple a template with a custom tag. This would possibly eleminate the need for `<apply />`. For example:\n  `<template is=\"name-element\" data-name>... </template>`\n  `<name-element data-name=\"rdewilde\" />`\n\nGL!\n",
